\subsection{Stator Slot Dimensions}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The stator slot dimensions are selected based on the required winding
arrangement, slot area, conductor accommodation, and magnetic loading.
The proposed PMSM consists of 24 stator slots, three phases, and four
poles. A double-layer distributed winding is considered for the
machine.

The principal dimensions of the stator slot are shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:stator_slot_dimensions}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{stator_slot.png}
    \caption{Stator slot dimensions of the proposed PMSM}
    \label{fig:stator_slot_dimensions}
\end{figure}

The main slot parameters considered in the geometrical model are
shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:stator_slot_dimensions}. These parameters
include the slot opening width $b_{s0}$, slot opening height $h_{s0}$,
slot neck height $h_{s1}$, slot neck width $b_{s1}$, slot body width
$b_{s2}$, slot body depth $h_{s2}$, stator tooth width $w_{st}$, and
stator yoke width $w_{sy}$.

\subsubsection*{Stator Slot Pitch}

The number of stator slots is

\begin{equation}
Q_s = 24.
\end{equation}

The air-gap diameter obtained from the preliminary machine design is

\begin{equation}
D = 57.15~\mathrm{mm}.
\end{equation}

The mechanical slot pitch is calculated as

\begin{equation}
\tau_s =
\frac{\pi D}{Q_s}.
\end{equation}

Substituting the values,

\begin{equation}
\tau_s =
\frac{\pi(57.15)}{24}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\tau_s = 7.481~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}

The mechanical angular displacement between two adjacent stator slots
is

\begin{equation}
\theta_s =
\frac{360^\circ}{Q_s}.
\end{equation}

Hence,

\begin{equation}
\theta_s =
\frac{360^\circ}{24}
=
\boxed{15^\circ}.
\end{equation}


%---------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection*{Stator Tooth Width}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The average air-gap magnetic loading selected for the machine is

\begin{equation}
B_{\mathrm{av}} = 0.95~\mathrm{T}.
\end{equation}

The active stack length obtained from the preliminary design is

\begin{equation}
L = 89.78~\mathrm{mm}.
\end{equation}

The number of pole pairs is

\begin{equation}
p = 2.
\end{equation}

The flux per pole is calculated from

\begin{equation}
\Phi_p =
B_{\mathrm{av}}
\frac{\pi D L}{2p}.
\end{equation}

Substituting the design values,

\begin{equation}
\Phi_p =
0.95
\frac{\pi(0.05715)(0.08978)}
{2(2)}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\Phi_p = 3.83~\mathrm{mWb}
}
\end{equation}

The number of stator slots per pole is

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{sp}} =
\frac{Q_s}{2p}
=
\frac{24}{4}
=
6.
\end{equation}

The preliminary stator tooth flux density is selected as

\begin{equation}
B_{st}=1.6~\mathrm{T}.
\end{equation}

The tooth width is estimated from

\begin{equation}
w_{st} =
\frac{\Phi_p/N_{\mathrm{sp}}}
{B_{st}L}.
\end{equation}

Substituting the values,

\begin{equation}
w_{st} =
\frac{3.83\times10^{-3}/6}
{1.6(0.08978)}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
w_{st} \approx 4.44~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}


%---------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection*{Slot Opening Dimensions}
%---------------------------------------------------------

A relatively small slot opening is selected to reduce the slot leakage
and maintain an adequate air-gap permeance distribution. The
preliminary slot opening width is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
b_{s0}=1.0~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}

and the slot opening height is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
h_{s0}=1.0~\mathrm{mm}.
}
\end{equation}

The slot neck height is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
h_{s1}=1.5~\mathrm{mm}.
}
\end{equation}

Therefore, the total height of the slot opening and neck is

\begin{equation}
h_{s0}+h_{s1}
=
1.0+1.5
=
\boxed{2.5~\mathrm{mm}}.
\end{equation}


%---------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection*{Slot Neck Width}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The slot neck width is calculated from the slot geometry as

\begin{equation}
b_{s1}
=
2\left[
\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{Q_s}\right)
\left(
\frac{D}{2}+h_{s0}+h_{s1}
\right)
-
\frac{w_{st}/2}
{\cos(\pi/Q_s)}
\right].
\end{equation}

Using

\begin{equation}
D=57.15~\mathrm{mm},
\qquad
Q_s=24,
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
h_{s0}=1.0~\mathrm{mm},
\qquad
h_{s1}=1.5~\mathrm{mm},
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
w_{st}=4.44~\mathrm{mm},
\end{equation}

the slot neck width becomes

\begin{equation}
b_{s1}
=
2\left[
\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{24}\right)
(28.575+1.0+1.5)
-
\frac{4.44/2}
{\cos(\pi/24)}
\right].
\end{equation}

Thus,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
b_{s1}\approx3.70~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}


%---------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection*{Stator Yoke Thickness}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The stator yoke thickness is estimated from the required yoke flux
density. A preliminary stator yoke flux density of

\begin{equation}
B_{sy}=1.6~\mathrm{T}
\end{equation}

is selected.

The required yoke thickness is estimated using

\begin{equation}
h_{sy}
=
\frac{\Phi_p}
{2B_{sy}L}.
\end{equation}

Substituting the values,

\begin{equation}
h_{sy}
=
\frac{3.83\times10^{-3}}
{2(1.6)(0.08978)}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
h_{sy}\approx13.33~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}


%---------------------------------------------------------
\subsubsection*{Slot Body Dimensions}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The slot body dimensions are selected to provide sufficient space for
the required stator conductors while maintaining an adequate stator
tooth width and yoke thickness.

A preliminary slot body depth is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
h_{s2}=14.0~\mathrm{mm}
}
\end{equation}

and the preliminary slot body width is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
b_{s2}=4.5~\mathrm{mm}.
}
\end{equation}

These values are preliminary geometrical dimensions and can be
further optimized after determining the required number of conductors,
current density, copper area, and slot fill factor.


