\subsection{Determination of Number of Turns per Coil}
%---------------------------------------------------------

The number of turns per coil is determined from the RMS back-EMF
equation of the PMSM. The rated supply voltage is considered as

\begin{equation}
V_{\mathrm{ph}} = 230~\mathrm{V_{rms}}.
\end{equation}

The required ratio between the back EMF and the applied voltage is
assumed to be

\begin{equation}
\frac{E_{\mathrm{ph}}}{V_{\mathrm{ph}}}=0.91.
\end{equation}

Therefore, the required RMS back EMF is

\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{ph}}
=
0.91V_{\mathrm{ph}}
=
0.91(230)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
E_{\mathrm{ph}}=209.3~\mathrm{V_{rms}}
}
\end{equation}

For a sinusoidal PMSM, the RMS back-EMF equation is

\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{ph}}
=
4.44 f_e \Phi_p N_{\mathrm{ph}} k_w
\end{equation}

where $f_e$ is the electrical frequency, $\Phi_p$ is the flux per
pole, $N_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is the number of series turns per phase, and
$k_w$ is the winding factor.

For the proposed machine,

\begin{equation}
f_e=66.67~\mathrm{Hz},
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\Phi_p=3.83\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{Wb},
\end{equation}

and

\begin{equation}
k_w=0.983.
\end{equation}

Hence, the required number of series turns per phase is

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{ph}}
=
\frac{E_{\mathrm{ph}}}
{4.44 f_e\Phi_p k_w}.
\end{equation}

Substituting the above values,

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{ph}}
=
\frac{209.3}
{4.44(66.67)(3.83\times10^{-3})(0.983)}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
N_{\mathrm{ph}}\approx188~\mathrm{turns/phase}
}
\end{equation}

The proposed machine has 24 stator slots and a double-layer winding.
Therefore, the total number of coils is 24, giving

\begin{equation}
N_{c,\mathrm{ph}}
=
\frac{24}{3}
=
8~\mathrm{coils/phase}.
\end{equation}

The theoretical number of turns per coil is consequently

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{coil}}
=
\frac{N_{\mathrm{ph}}}{N_{c,\mathrm{ph}}}
=
\frac{188}{8}
\approx23.5.
\end{equation}

Since the number of turns must be an integer, the number of turns per
coil is selected as

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
N_{\mathrm{coil}}=24~\mathrm{turns/coil}
}
\end{equation}

Thus, the actual number of series turns per phase becomes

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{ph,actual}}
=
24\times8
=
\boxed{192~\mathrm{turns/phase}}.
\end{equation}

The resulting back EMF corresponding to 24 turns per coil is

\begin{equation}
E_{\mathrm{ph,actual}}
=
4.44(66.67)(3.83\times10^{-3})(192)(0.983)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
E_{\mathrm{ph,actual}}\approx214~\mathrm{V_{rms}}
}
\end{equation}

Thus, the initial winding design for the PMSM uses

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
24~\mathrm{turns/coil}
}
\end{equation}

with 8 coils per phase and 192 series turns per phase.


\subsubsection{Conductors per Slot}

Since the machine uses a double-layer winding, each stator slot
contains two coil sides. For the selected winding design,

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{coil}}=24~\mathrm{turns/coil}.
\end{equation}

Each turn consists of two active conductor sides. Therefore, the number
of conductors in one coil side is

\begin{equation}
Z_{\mathrm{side}}
=
2N_{\mathrm{coil}}
=
2(24)
=
48.
\end{equation}

Since two coil sides are accommodated in each slot, the total number of
conductors per slot is

\begin{equation}
Z_{\mathrm{slot}}
=
2Z_{\mathrm{side}}
=
2(48)
=
\boxed{96~\mathrm{conductors/slot}}.
\end{equation}

Thus, the selected winding contains

\begin{equation}
\boxed{Z_{\mathrm{slot}}=96~\mathrm{conductors/slot}}.
\end{equation}

