\subsection{Slot Area, Conductor Area and Slot Fill Factor}
%---------------------------------------------------------

According to the winding design, the PMSM has 24 stator slots, 24
coils, and a double-layer distributed winding. The number of coils per
phase is

\begin{equation}
N_{c,\mathrm{ph}}  = \frac{24}{3} =8~\mathrm{coils/phase}.
\end{equation}

Two parallel paths are used for each phase. From the SPWM design, the
phase current is

\begin{equation}
I_{\mathrm{ph}}=10.11~\mathrm{A}.
\end{equation}

Therefore, the current carried by each parallel path is

\begin{equation}
I_{\mathrm{path}}=
\frac{I_{\mathrm{ph}}}{a}=
\frac{10.11}{2}=
\boxed{5.055~\mathrm{A}}.
\end{equation}

A copper current density of

\begin{equation}
J=5~\mathrm{A/mm^2}
\end{equation}

is selected. Hence, the required cross-sectional area of one
conductor is

\begin{equation}
A_{\mathrm{cond}}
=
\frac{I_{\mathrm{path}}}{J}
=
\frac{5.055}{5}
=
\boxed{1.011~\mathrm{mm^2}}.
\end{equation}

The selected number of turns per coil is

\begin{equation}
N_{\mathrm{coil}}=24~\mathrm{turns/coil}.
\end{equation}

Since a double-layer winding is used, each slot contains two coil
sides. Therefore, the number of conductor cross-sections per slot is

\begin{equation}
Z_{\mathrm{slot}}
=
2N_{\mathrm{coil}}
=
2(24)
=
\boxed{48}.
\end{equation}

The total copper area required in one stator slot is

\begin{equation}
A_{\mathrm{cu,slot}}
=
Z_{\mathrm{slot}}A_{\mathrm{cond}}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
A_{\mathrm{cu,slot}}
=
48(1.011)
=
\boxed{48.528~\mathrm{mm^2}}.
\end{equation}

The gross slot area obtained from the selected stator slot geometry is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{slot,gross}}
=
69.55~\mathrm{mm^2}
}.
\end{equation}

The slot fill factor is defined as

\begin{equation}
k_{\mathrm{fill}}
=
\frac{A_{\mathrm{cu,slot}}}
{A_{\mathrm{slot,gross}}}.
\end{equation}

Therefore,

\begin{equation}
k_{\mathrm{fill}}
=
\frac{48.528}{69.55}
=
0.6979.
\end{equation}

Hence, the slot fill factor is

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
k_{\mathrm{fill}}=69.79\%
}.
\end{equation}

The resulting winding and slot parameters are

\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
I_{\mathrm{ph}} &= 10.11~\mathrm{A},&
I_{\mathrm{path}} &= 5.055~\mathrm{A},\\
a &= 2,&
J &= 5~\mathrm{A/mm^2},\\
A_{\mathrm{cond}} &= 1.011~\mathrm{mm^2},&
N_{\mathrm{coil}} &= 24~\mathrm{turns/coil},\\
Z_{\mathrm{slot}} &= 48,&
A_{\mathrm{cu,slot}} &= 48.528~\mathrm{mm^2},\\
A_{\mathrm{slot,gross}} &= 69.55~\mathrm{mm^2},&
k_{\mathrm{fill}} &= 69.79\%.
\end{aligned}
}
\end{equation}

\section{Material Assignment}

The appropriate materials were assigned to the different components of the radial flux Surface-Mounted Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motor (SPMSM) model in Ansys Maxwell. The stator core was assigned M250-35A electrical steel, which is suitable for laminated magnetic cores. NdFeB permanent magnet material was assigned to the rotor magnets to provide the required excitation field. Copper was assigned to the stator windings because of its high electrical conductivity. The remaining non-magnetic regions were assigned suitable air/vacuum material.

The materials used in the electromagnetic model are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:material_assignment}.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Material Assignment}
\label{tab:material_assignment}
\begin{tabular}{|l|l|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Assigned Material} \\ \hline
Stator Core & Cogent Power M250-35A \\ \hline
Rotor Permanent Magnets & Arnold Magnetics N42EH \\ \hline
Stator Windings & Copper \\ \hline
Air Region & Vacuum \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The material properties were defined in the Ansys Maxwell material library according to the requirements of the electromagnetic analysis. The electrical steel was specified with its magnetic B-H characteristics, while the permanent magnet was defined using its magnetic properties. Copper was assigned its electrical conductivity for the winding region..

\section{Boundary Conditions}

Boundary conditions were applied to define the electromagnetic behavior at the outer boundary of the computational region. In the present Ansys Maxwell 2D transient model, a Vector Potential boundary condition was assigned to the outer boundary of the solution region. This boundary condition limits the magnetic field at the external boundary and allows the electromagnetic field to be solved within the motor region. The boundary was selected sufficiently far from the active machine geometry to minimize its influence on the calculated electromagnetic quantities.

\section{Excitations}

The stator windings were excited using a balanced three-phase sinusoidal voltage supply. Separate winding excitations were assigned to Phase A, Phase B, and Phase C in Ansys Maxwell. The voltage applied to Phase A was defined as

\begin{equation}
V_A(t)=\sqrt{2}\,V_{\mathrm{ph,rms}}
\sin(2\pi f t)
\end{equation}

The remaining phase voltages were displaced by $120^\circ$ electrically and are given by

\begin{align}
V_B(t) &=
\sqrt{2}\,V_{\mathrm{ph,rms}}
\sin\left(2\pi f t-\frac{2\pi}{3}\right),\\
V_C(t) &=
\sqrt{2}\,V_{\mathrm{ph,rms}}
\sin\left(2\pi f t+\frac{2\pi}{3}\right).
\end{align}

In the Maxwell model, the winding excitation was specified as a voltage excitation with an initial current of zero. The winding resistance was set to $1.5~\Omega$, while the number of parallel branches was set to one. The same excitation approach was applied to the three phases to obtain a balanced rotating magnetic field in the stator.

The three-phase excitation produces the rotating magnetic field required for synchronous operation of the surface-mounted permanent magnet rotor. The resulting interaction between the stator magnetic field and the permanent magnet field produces the electromagnetic torque of the motor.
